userStories.description:
**Goal** — Extend implementer questions with protected-path/command facts and fixed config-change menu options while preserving string answers.

**Motivation** — The advisor's question carries only the implementer's concern text; nothing tells it which paths are protected.

**Approach** — **Advisor menu, not free text.** The advisor's menus already carry parameters the advisor never chooses (`AdviceOptionFixed`). nax builds one `config-change` option per allowed change and the advisor picks one by id and states a `reason`. The advisor cannot name a file, a key or a command that is not on the menu.

`packages/nax/src/advisor/menus.ts`: `LABELS` gains `"config-change": "Change a nax command to a script the package declares"`; `REQUIRED_TEXT_FIELD["config-change"]` is `"reason"`; `buildMenu` builds the implementer-question menu as `answer`, `hold`, then one `config-change` option per candidate, in candidate order, with `fixed.configChange` set and label `Change quality.commands.<key> to "<to>" (package.json script "<script>")`; `toAction` maps a `config-change` option to `{ type: "config-change", ...option.fixed.configChange, reason: reply.reason }`.

`packages/nax/src/operations/advise.ts`: the `REQUIRED` mirror gains `"config-change": "reason"`. `packages/nax/src/advisor/decision-log.ts`: `actionText` gains a `config-change` case returning `config-change quality.commands.<key> -> "<to>"`. `AdviceEvidence.source` gains `"config"`.

When `q.facts` is given, the question's evidence gains, after the concern entry, one `source: "config"` entry with one line per protected path (`Protected (no session can write): <path>`) and one `source: "config"` entry with one line per key in `lint`, `typecheck`, `testScoped`, `test` order (`quality.commands.<key>: <command>`, or `quality.commands.<key>: (unset: nax detects a default)`); the menu is built with `configChanges: q.facts.candidates`. When `q.facts` is absent no `facts`-derived evidence or option is added. A `config-change` decision returns a `ConfigChangeAnswer` from the action's fields plus the decision's `id` and `needsHumanConfirm`.

A `config-change` decision whose `needsHumanConfirm` is true is not applied (refusal `needs-confirm`). That flag is set by the advisor or forced by `forcedConfirm` (`advisor/menus.ts`) when the reply's `confidence` is `"low"` or `reversible` is false; `forcedConfirm` is not changed, so a low-confidence or non-reversible pick is refused too. Any other `config-change` decision is applied by later stories, not this one.

**Scope** — In: advisor types, menus, validation mirror, decision-log text, workflow advisor interface and live facts/result mapping; extend existing unit suites. Keep the existing absorbTurn caller type-safe by narrowing string results pending US-005. Out: computing candidates (US-002), writing/reloading config (US-003/US-004), loop application (US-005), early stop/status (US-006), residual-menu changes and restrictions on free-text answers. Feature boundary: Validating or rewriting advisor answer text that tells the implementer to edit a protected path (#2897's alternative fix): the advisor gets the facts, not a new restriction.

**Interface** —
```typescript
type QualityCommandKey = "lint" | "typecheck" | "testScoped" | "test";
interface ConfigChangeCandidate { key: QualityCommandKey; to: string; script: string }
// AdviceAction gains:
type ConfigChangeAction = { type: "config-change"; key: QualityCommandKey; to: string; script: string; reason: string };
// AdviceOptionFixed gains:
interface ConfigChangeFixed { configChange?: ConfigChangeCandidate }
// MenuFacts implementer-question variant:
type ImplementerQuestionMenuFacts = { kind: "implementer-question"; configChanges?: readonly ConfigChangeCandidate[] };
interface ConfigChangeAnswer { kind: "config-change"; key: QualityCommandKey; to: string; script: string; reason: string; decisionId: string; needsHumanConfirm: boolean }
// AC17 requires arrays at this boundary; this widens the prose's string-only commands declaration.
interface ImplementerQuestionFacts { protectedPaths: readonly string[]; commands: Readonly<Partial<Record<QualityCommandKey, string | readonly string[]>>>; candidates: readonly ConfigChangeCandidate[] }
// WorkflowAdvisor method:
answerQuestion(q: { storyId: string; status: "NEEDS_CONTEXT" | "BLOCKED"; concerns: string; facts?: ImplementerQuestionFacts }): Promise<string | ConfigChangeAnswer | null>;
```
A string is still an answer. Render array facts as elements joined with ` && `, not JSON; the shared renderCommandSpec module is introduced by US-002, so do not create a forward dependency on that file here.

**Goal** — Add orchestrator-owned transactional config application and unconditional PackageRegistry.refresh.

**Motivation** — Per-package config is resolved once per run, so even the hand edit (store-erp `d55cafb9`) only reached the next run.

**Approach** — **The config file** is `.nax/mono/<pkg>/config.json`. The advisor never chooses it.

**Who applies, with which signals.** A `config-change` decision whose `needsHumanConfirm` is true is not applied (refusal `needs-confirm`). That flag is set by the advisor or forced by `forcedConfirm` (`advisor/menus.ts`) when the reply's `confidence` is `"low"` or `reversible` is false; `forcedConfirm` is not changed, so a low-confidence or non-reversible pick is refused too. Any other `config-change` decision is applied.

**Applying** (`applyConfigChange`, run by nax, never by a session), in order:

1. Refuse `needs-confirm` when the decision needs confirmation; refuse `root-package` when `<pkg>` is the repo root.
2. Recompute the candidates (same rule, same inputs) and refuse `not-offered` unless the change's key and value match one exactly.
3. Refuse `untracked` unless the config file is tracked in git (`git ls-files --error-unmatch -- <file>` exits 0).
4. Read the file, set `quality.commands.<key>` to the value without changing other keys (an absent `quality` or `commands` object is created), and write it back as 2-space-indented JSON with a trailing newline. A write that throws is refused `write-failed`.
5. Clear the per-package config cache (`packageConfigCache.clear()`), resolve the story's config again with `loadConfigForPackage(projectDir, pkg, currentConfig)` (which keeps the run's `--profile` chain and root-only keys), and refresh the registry entry with `registry.refresh(pkg)`. If either throws, restore the file's original bytes, clear the cache and refresh again, and refuse `invalid-config`.
6. Commit only that path: `git commit --no-verify -m "chore(nax): set quality.commands.<key> for <pkg> (advisor <decisionId>)" -- <file>`, mirroring `commitLedger`. If the commit fails, restore the original bytes, clear the cache and refresh again, and refuse `commit-failed`.
7. Return the new resolved config and the refreshed package view.

`PackageRegistry` gains `refresh(packageDir: string, loadOverride?: PackageOverrideLoader): Promise<PackageView>`, which re-runs the package's hydration unconditionally (re-reading its override), drops its cached views, and returns `resolve(packageDir)`. Preserve the existing override-key/worktree identity distinction and root-only/profile behavior. A file write throwing requires original bytes restored if the file changed. Resolving OR registry refresh throwing requires restoration, cache clear and registry refresh from original bytes. A failed application returns neither config nor packageView. Extend the US-002 dependency object for deterministic fault injection; no agent/model checks authorize this write.

**Scope** — In: extend config-change.ts, refresh API/implementation, candidate revalidation, seven refusal reasons, JSON update/restore, profile-preserving reload, cache invalidation and single-path git commit; create integration tests and extend registry tests. Out: env context replacement and sink persistence (US-004), turn handling/ledger (US-005), early-stop/status (US-006), root config writes and session permission changes. Feature boundaries: A model (approver agent) judging whether a config change is safe: the allowed changes are fixed by the rule and the advisor only picks one. Config changes for a story whose package is the repo root (including every story of a single-package repo): the root config is cached for the whole run, so such a change could only reach the next run. Changing `RequestCapability` or the session write block on nax config.

**Interface** —
```typescript
interface ApplyConfigChangeInput { projectDir: string; packageDir: string /* "" for the repo root */; base: string; change: ConfigChangeAnswer; currentConfig: NaxConfig; registry: PackageRegistry }
type ApplyConfigChangeResult = { outcome: ConfigChangeOutcome; config?: NaxConfig; packageView?: PackageView };
type ConfigChangeOutcome = { applied: true; key: QualityCommandKey; from: string | undefined; to: string; commit: string } | { applied: false; key: QualityCommandKey; to: string; reason: ConfigChangeRefusal };
type ConfigChangeRefusal = "needs-confirm" | "root-package" | "not-offered" | "untracked" | "invalid-config" | "write-failed" | "commit-failed";
function applyConfigChange(input: ApplyConfigChangeInput): Promise<ApplyConfigChangeResult>;
// PackageRegistry gains:
refresh(packageDir: string, loadOverride?: PackageOverrideLoader): Promise<PackageView>;
const _configChangeDeps = { git, loadConfigForPackage, readFile, writeFile };
```
`config` and `packageView` present only when applied. Render `from` with renderCommandSpec (arrays joined with ` && `). Preserve the specified input signature: revalidation uses currentConfig, base scripts and the gate mapping for the selected key, without requiring a new redGates parameter; the caller constructs the real red-gate menu. Mirror commitLedger's single-path add/diff/commit pattern, never commit all staged edits.

**Goal** — Make buildStoryEnv provide config facts/application steps and append-only persistence, replacing the story's captured config/view so the next gates run the changed command.

**Motivation** — Nothing in a run can change nax config.

**Approach** — **Making the story use it.** Gate ops read their command through `callCtx.packageView.select(...)` (`operations/verify-scoped.ts`, `operations/lint-check.ts`), so the refreshed package view is what changes the gate command. `buildStoryEnv` passes `packageDirRelative(projectDir, workdir) ?? ""` as `packageDir` and `callCtx.runtime.packages` as `registry`. It holds the story's step context in a mutable holder (`let current: StepContext`), and every step closure reads `current`. After an applied change it replaces `current` with `{ ...current, config: newConfig, callCtx: { ...current.callCtx, config: newConfig, packageView: newView } }`. Later stories in the run resolve their config through the cleared cache and the refreshed registry. Only the gate path must observe the new command within the story (an AC pins it); whether other step closures (story review, the model lookup) read `current` is left to the builder.

`configFacts` builds protected paths from protectedPathList(naxProtectedPaths()), renders the story's resolved quality command specs, reads scripts at the original sc.base using readBaseScripts, and calls configChangeCandidates for the supplied redGates with raw command specs (not rendered arrays). For the repo-root package return no candidates. A missing base scripts object still returns protectedPaths and commands, with candidates []. `applyConfigChange` delegates to US-003 with projectDir/packageDir/base/currentConfig/registry, replaces current only after success, and returns its outcome.

`buildStoryEnv`'s sink appends the entry to the story record's `configChanges` (keeping earlier entries, including ones from a resumed prior record) and persists the record. The shallow `update` patch is not used for this field, so a second change never replaces the first. Introduce the shared record typing needed by this sink now; US-005 will produce records from turn outcomes. All new methods remain optional for existing env callers/harnesses.

**Scope** — In: stage-env wiring, mutable current holder, optional step/sink contracts, minimal shared ConfigChangeRecord and optional WorkflowStoryRecord.configChanges fields, real next-gate integration tests, and the refresh behavior regression test specified here. Out: candidate algorithm/application transaction (US-002/US-003), absorbing advisor answers or writing outcome ledger entries (US-005), early-stop and CLI rendering (US-006); no root-package application or session write permission changes. Feature boundary: Config changes for a story whose package is the repo root (including every story of a single-package repo): the root config is cached for the whole run, so such a change could only reach the next run.

**Interface** —
```typescript
// StoryLoopSteps gains:
configFacts?(redGates: readonly GateName[]): Promise<ImplementerQuestionFacts>;
applyConfigChange?(change: ConfigChangeAnswer): Promise<ConfigChangeOutcome>;
// StoryLoopSink gains:
recordConfigChange?(entry: ConfigChangeRecord): Promise<void>;
interface ConfigChangeRecord { round: number; key: QualityCommandKey; from?: string; to: string; applied: boolean; reason?: ConfigChangeRefusal; decisionId: string }
// WorkflowStoryRecord gains:
configChanges?: readonly ConfigChangeRecord[];
```
Use the types exported by upstream stories; no file created in this story belongs in contextFiles. Preserve existing resume/token/timing/closeOnError behavior and the caller's package execution identity.

**Goal** — Extend absorbTurn to collect red-gate facts, apply config-change answers, record every outcome and mark successful turns without spending another implementer turn.

**Motivation** — The implementer proved the fix (272 pass / 0 fail in 4.9 s with `--isolate`) and replied `BLOCKED`, because nax config writes are denied to every session by design.

**Approach** — The change is proposed and applied inside the round in which the implementer replies `BLOCKED` or `NEEDS_CONTEXT`. That is the only place where an applied change is followed by another gate run without changing the loop's state machine: `absorbTurn` (`packages/nax/src/workflow/story-steps.ts`) already continues the round after the advisor answers, and the next `evaluateStep` re-runs the gates. The residual path is not used: `residualStep` filters out gate items (`f.kind !== "gate"`) and is followed by `closeStep`, so a change ruled there would never be re-checked.

**After an applied change, in the loop.** The turn ends without another implementer turn (the answer was the config change). `lastTurn` records the `BLOCKED` (or `NEEDS_CONTEXT`) reply with `configApplied: true`; the report check reads it as for any such turn. The round stays spent: a config-only round counts against `maxFixRounds`. The config commit moves HEAD, so `fixDiffEmpty` reads the round as not empty and a re-review runs when review findings are open.

`absorbTurn` (reached through `implementStep` and `fixStep`) passes facts to the advisor when gates are red, applies a picked config change through `env.steps.applyConfigChange` and ends the turn without another implementer turn, and records each change through `env.sink.recordConfigChange`, the ledger and `lastTurn.configApplied`. The red gates are the gates whose state is `"red"` in the story loop's last gate run (`gates.states` only; the full-suite result `suite.states` is not read). With no red gate there are no candidates. Keep the existing question-count limit. If no gate run exists, no gates are red, or configFacts is absent, call answerQuestion with the original exact object and no facts key. A throwing configFacts is logged warn and the question is asked without facts.

If an application is refused, write the refusal ledger line, append an unapplied config-change record with its reason, and record the implementer's concern including the proposed command, then continue the existing loop path. If applyConfigChange throws, treat it as refused write-failed; do not let that throw escape fixStep. A string answer still triggers the existing answer turn; null still records the concern. Optional steps/sink are compatibility seams and must not turn a missing applier into a successful application.

Applied ledger: `config change applied: quality.commands.<key> "<from>" -> "<to>" (advisor <decisionId>)`. Refused ledger: `config change refused (<reason>): quality.commands.<key> -> "<to>" (advisor <decisionId>)`. Carry from/to/key/round/applied/reason/decisionId into the sink record, preserving prior records through the append sink introduced by US-004.

**Scope** — In: absorbTurn branching/facts/failure containment, outcome ledger and sink records, recordLastTurn and optional configApplied on the existing state carrier. Out: transaction and env implementation (US-003/US-004), evaluateStep early stop and CLI output (US-006); no residual config menu, graph key/produces changes, first-implement candidates or extra report turn. Feature boundaries: Offering or applying a config change at residual time, or adding gate items to the residual menu. Offering config changes before the story's first gate run (the first implement turn has no red gates, so no candidates).

**Interface** —
```typescript
// StoryLoopState.lastTurn extends the existing carrier:
lastTurn?: { readonly status: ImplementerStatus; readonly fallback: boolean; readonly stepUps: number; readonly configApplied?: boolean };
// Consume the optional methods provided by US-004:
configFacts?(redGates: readonly GateName[]): Promise<ImplementerQuestionFacts>;
applyConfigChange?(change: ConfigChangeAnswer): Promise<ConfigChangeOutcome>;
recordConfigChange?(entry: ConfigChangeRecord): Promise<void>;
interface ConfigChangeRecord { round: number; key: QualityCommandKey; from?: string; to: string; applied: boolean; reason?: ConfigChangeRefusal; decisionId: string }
// WorkflowStoryRecord field remains optional:
configChanges?: readonly ConfigChangeRecord[];
```
The shared record type/field already exists from US-004 and is consumed here. A successful application does not create a new concern. Preserve fallback and stepUps from the original reply, the report-check behavior for BLOCKED/NEEDS_CONTEXT and STORY_GRAPH's existing produces lists.
